# =====================================================================
# routeur-tat-nguy-1 - Part 2 - DYNAMIC MULTICAST (group) VXLAN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# eth0    : UNDERLAY, cabled to Switch-tat-nguy, IP 10.1.1.1/24.
#           The "real" network used to carry the tunnel.
# eth1    : cabled to host-tat-nguy-1. NO IP: it is only a port of
#           the bridge br0 (Layer 2, like a switch port).
# vxlan10 : the VTEP. Wraps host Ethernet frames in UDP/IP packets
#           (VNI 10, UDP 4789) and unwraps packets from the other VTEP.
# br0     : Linux bridge = software switch joining eth1 and vxlan10.
#
# MULTICAST mode: no peer is written by hand. Each VTEP joins the
# multicast group 239.1.1.1 on eth0 (via IGMP). Broadcast traffic
# (e.g. ARP) is sent to the group, so EVERY VTEP of VNI 10 gets it.
# When the answer comes back, each VTEP learns "this MAC is behind
# that VTEP IP" and then uses unicast (flood-and-learn). Scales better:
# adding a VTEP only requires joining the group.
#
# Result: the router acts like one half of a switch stretched over
# the underlay network. VXLAN adds ~50 bytes of headers, so vxlan10
# gets MTU 1450 (1500 - 50) automatically.
# =====================================================================

# Underlay: give eth0 an IP to reach the other VTEP
ip addr add 10.1.1.1/24 dev eth0
ip link set eth0 up

# Multicast group instead of a fixed remote peer
ip link add name vxlan10 type vxlan id 10 dev eth0 group 239.1.1.1 dstport 4789
ip link set vxlan10 up

# Bridge joining host-facing port and tunnel
ip link add br0 type bridge
ip link set br0 up
ip link set eth1 up
brctl addif br0 eth1
# the same with `ip link set eth1 master br0`
brctl addif br0 vxlan10
# = `ip link set vxlan10 master br0`